A \emph{fold} is one split of the data into \emph{analysis} rows, which fit the
model, and \emph{assessment} rows, which are held out and scored. An
\emph{assessment occurrence} is one held-out row in one fold. A row assessed in
two folds has two occurrences.
}

\section{Read these elements first}{
\itemize{
\item \code{complete}: \code{TRUE} when every planned fold was fitted and scored. When
any fold fails it is \code{FALSE}, and every pooled score in \code{summary} is
\code{NA}. Never rank a model on the folds that happened to finish.
\item \code{summary}: data frame of pooled scores, one row per metric.
\itemize{
\item \code{metric} names the score, and \code{estimate} is the pooled score (lower
is better).
\item \code{unit} says what one score refers to, \code{"observation"} or, for the
Brier score, \code{"trial"}.
\item \code{n_assessment} is the number of planned assessment occurrences, and
\code{n_scored} the number that entered the score.
\item \code{weight_sum} is the total frequency weight behind the score
(frequency weight x trials for the Brier score).
\item \code{complete} repeats the completion flag.
}
\item \code{metrics}: the same columns plus \code{fold}, with one row per fold and
metric. A fold that finished keeps its scores even when another fold
failed. The pooled RMSE is not the mean of the fold RMSEs.
\item \code{fold_results}: data frame with \code{fold}, \code{n_analysis} and \code{n_assessment}
(rows used to fit and to score), and \code{status}, either \code{"scored"} or
\code{"failed"}.
\item \code{errors}: list with one element per fold. It is \code{NULL} for a scored
fold. For a failed fold it holds \code{stage}, where the fold stopped (for
example \code{"initial_glm"}, \code{"scale_fit"}, \code{"prediction"}, or
\code{"training_selection"} in a \code{\link[=select_cv]{select_cv()}} result), and \code{message}, the
reason. A failed scale fit can also carry \code{start_results} or
\code{radius_profile}. Failed folds are never retried.
\item \code{warnings}: list with one character vector per fold, holding the warning
messages captured while that fold was fitted and predicted. They are
captured rather than printed, so read them here.
}
}

\section{Read the scores}{
Every score is a loss, so lower is better. Each assessment occurrence gets
its own loss, stored in a column of \code{predictions} named after the metric.
The pooled \code{estimate} is the frequency-weighted mean of those losses over
the eligible occurrences. An occurrence is eligible when its frequency
weight is positive and, for binomial data, it has at least one trial.
Ineligible occurrences stay in \code{predictions} with \code{eligible = FALSE} and
contribute nothing.\tabular{lll}{
   Metric \tab Loss for one occurrence \tab Pooled score and its scale \cr
   \code{rmse} \tab Squared error, (observed - predicted)^2 \tab Square root of the mean squared error, in response units (for example, animals per survey) \cr
   \code{mae} \tab Absolute error \tab Mean absolute error, in response units \cr
   \code{neg_log_score} \tab Negative natural log of the probability (counts, binomial successes) or density (Gamma) that the fitted model gives the observed value \tab Mean per observation, in natural-log units \cr
   \code{deviance} \tab Poisson deviance contribution (Poisson only) \tab Mean per observation \cr
   \code{brier} \tab Squared error of the success probability, averaged over the row's trials (binomial only) \tab Mean per trial, between 0 and 1 \cr
}

\subsection{How to read the scores}{
\itemize{
\item RMSE and MAE judge only the predicted mean. RMSE weighs large misses
heavily, and MAE treats every miss in proportion to its size. Note that
the \code{rmse} column of \code{predictions} holds squared errors, not their roots.
\item The negative log score judges the whole predicted distribution, so it
also penalizes a model that is too confident. It keeps every constant of
the probability function, such as the binomial coefficient. A
difference of D between two models means the better one gave the
observed outcomes exp(D) times the probability, as a geometric mean per
observation. A difference of 0.1 is a ratio of about 1.11. For Gamma
responses the score is a density, so it depends on the response units
and can be negative.
\item An observed outcome given zero probability produces an infinite log
score. The value is kept, not clipped.
\item For NB2 and Gamma, the log score uses theta or the dispersion estimated
from the analysis rows and frozen for scoring.
\item A score means little alone. Compare candidates fitted to the same folds
with \code{\link[=cv_tab]{cv_tab()}}.
}
}
}

\section{Other elements}{
\itemize{
\item \code{predictions}: data frame with one row per assessment occurrence,
including the occurrences of failed folds, whose predictions and losses
are \code{NA}.
\itemize{
\item \code{fold} and \code{row_id} identify the occurrence.
\item \code{observed} is the response (the number of successes for binomial
data), and \code{trials} is 1 except for \code{cbind(successes, failures)}
responses.
\item \code{prediction} is the expected response on the response scale (the mean
count, the mean, or the probability of success per trial).
\item \code{weight} is the frequency weight, and \code{eligible} marks the
occurrences that enter the scores.
\item One loss column per metric is named after the metric.
\item NB2 and Gamma results add \code{distribution_family}, the frozen training
\code{theta} (NB2) or \code{dispersion} (Gamma), with \code{NA} where it does not
apply, and \code{poisson_limit}, which is \code{TRUE} only when a joint NB2 fit
verified the exact Poisson limit.
}
\item \code{training}: list with one element per fold, describing the fits to the
analysis rows. An element can be empty or partial when its fold failed
early.
\itemize{
\item \code{coefficients}: final regression coefficients on the link scale (log
for Poisson, NB2, and Gamma, logit for binomial, identity for
Gaussian). Use \code{exp()} on a log-link coefficient for a multiplicative
change in the expected response. Spatial covariates are standardized
with the training mean and SD, so their coefficients refer to a one
training SD change, unless \code{covariate_transform = "raw"} was used.
\item \code{initial_coefficients}: coefficients of the initial GLM, fitted at the
starting scales.
\item \code{scale_est}: data frame with \code{Mean} and \code{SE}, the estimated sigma and
its standard error in map units, one row per optimized covariate.
Similar values across folds suggest that the data identify the scales
well.
\item \code{shape_est}: estimated kernel shape (unitless) for kernels that have
one, otherwise \code{NULL}.
\item \code{initial_normalization}, \code{fitted_normalization}: lists of \code{mean} and
\code{sd}, the analysis-row centering and scaling of each spatial covariate
at the starting and the fitted scales. Assessment rows reuse them.
\item \code{bin_reference}: the frozen distance bins (\code{breaks}, representative
distances \code{bin_rep}, the extraction envelope \code{domain}, and
\code{cell_size}). Distances are in units of \code{max_D}, so 1 equals \code{max_D}.
\item \code{optimizer}: \code{par} (the optimized scale parameters on the internal
scale, sigma divided by \code{max_D}), \code{value} (the minimized objective),
\code{convergence} (0 means success), and \code{message}.
\item \code{start_results}: the table of optimizer starts from
\code{\link[=multiScale_optim]{multiScale_optim()}}, when available.
\item \code{initial_distribution} (NB2 and Gamma): the nuisance parameter of the
initial fit, in the same form as \code{distribution}.
}

\code{\link[=cross_validate_glm]{cross_validate_glm()}} estimates no scales, so its \code{training} holds
only \code{coefficients} (plus the NB2 and Gamma items).
\item \code{distribution} (NB2 and Gamma only): list with one element per fold,
holding the frozen training distribution. An element is \code{NULL} when the
training fit failed.
\itemize{
\item \code{family}, with \code{theta} (the NB2 size, \code{Inf} only for a verified
Poisson limit) or \code{dispersion} (the Gamma dispersion).
\item \code{poisson_limit}, and \code{estimated}, which is \code{FALSE} when the parameter
was fixed in advance.
\item \code{convention}, how the value was obtained, for example
\code{"native_gamma_deviance_scale"} (the training deviance divided by the
sum of the weights).
\item \code{rank}, \code{native_K}, \code{logLik}, \code{nobs}, \code{training_deviance}, and
\code{sum_weights} of the training fit.
}
\item \code{diagnostics}: list with one element per fold. \code{initial_glm} and
\code{fitted_glm} hold \code{converged}, \code{boundary}, and \code{iter} from the initial
and final GLM (NB2 and Gamma results also record \code{th.warn} and
\code{SE.theta} where the native fit has them).
\code{scale} holds the scale-fit diagnostics described in \code{\link[=diagnostics]{diagnostics()}}.
Check them before ranking models, because a scored fold is not
necessarily a trustworthy one.
\item \code{prediction_contracts}: list of the prediction contracts attached by
the prediction method of each fold (see \link{prediction_contract}). Each
states what the prediction conditions on and which uncertainty it
leaves out. It can be empty when the prediction method attaches none.
\item \code{folds}: the split plan as supplied.
\item \code{assessment_counts}: named integer vector, one value per input row,
giving how many times the row was assessed (0 for rows never held out).
\item \code{targets}: data frame with \code{row_id}, \code{observed}, \code{trials}, and \code{weight}
for every row used in any split, sorted by row ID. \code{\link[=cv_tab]{cv_tab()}} and
\code{\link[=select_cv]{select_cv()}} use it to confirm that candidates were scored on the same
data. It never enters a fit.
\item \code{selection_records}: \code{NULL} unless \code{keep_selection = TRUE}. Otherwise a
list with one compact record per fold, read by \code{\link[=select_cv]{select_cv()}}. Each
record holds \code{version}, \code{controls}, \code{diagnostics}, metadata \code{warnings},
\code{data}, and an \code{error} message if the record could not be built. Leave
the records unchanged. They cannot rebuild the fitted models. A
successful \code{data} holds these items.
\itemize{
\item \code{logLik}, the training log-likelihood (natural log).
\item \code{native_K}, the parameter count of the fitted regression as its
log-likelihood reports it, including an estimated dispersion or theta.
\item \code{ecological_K}, the number of estimated scales and shapes, and \code{K},
their total.
\item \code{nobs} and \code{observation_ids}, the rows that entered the likelihood.
\item \code{targets}, the analysis-row responses, trials, and weights, and
\code{signature}, a summary of the likelihood used to check that
candidates are comparable.
\item \code{formula} (as text), \code{family}, and \code{link}. NB2 and Gamma records add
\code{nuisance}.
}
\item \code{fits}: \code{NULL} unless \code{keep_fits = TRUE}. Otherwise the full model of
each fold, fitted to that fold's analysis rows only.
\item \code{raster_groups}: present only for inputs from
\code{\link[=kernel_prep_by_group]{kernel_prep_by_group()}}. For each fold, the map labels of the analysis
and assessment rows. These are fixed inputs, not fitted random effects.
\item \code{settings}: list with \code{family}, \code{link}, \code{backend} (\code{"glm"} for
\code{\link[=cross_validate_glm]{cross_validate_glm()}} and \code{"training_selection"} for \code{\link[=select_cv]{select_cv()}}),
\code{metrics}, the control lists used, and labels that state what the
scores mean. \code{normalization = "training_only"} and
\code{parameter_uncertainty = "not_integrated"}, for example, say that
standardization came from the analysis rows and that the predictions
treat fitted parameters as known. \code{\link[=cv_tab]{cv_tab()}} checks these labels
before comparing candidates.
\item \code{call}: the matched call.
}

A \code{\link[=select_cv]{select_cv()}} result also holds \code{selection}, with the \code{criterion} and
\code{ties} rule, the \code{candidates}, the \code{metrics} kept and \code{metrics_dropped},
\code{chosen} (the candidate selected in each fold, \code{NA} when selection
failed), \code{tables} (the training information-criterion table of each
fold), \code{candidate_settings}, \code{candidate_warnings}, and
\code{candidate_diagnostics}. Its \code{settings$controls_from} names the candidate
whose controls fill the shared \code{settings}. NB2 and Gamma selections add
\code{settings$fold_settings}, the settings of the model chosen in each fold.
}

\section{Functions that read it}{
\itemize{
\item \code{print()} reports how many folds were scored and how many input rows
were assessed, warns when the result is incomplete, and prints
\code{summary}.
\item \code{\link[=cv_tab]{cv_tab()}} ranks complete results on one score, after checking that
every candidate used identical folds, responses, trials, and weights.
\item \code{\link[=select_cv]{select_cv()}} scores a rule that picks a model in each fold by its
training AIC, AICc, or BIC. It needs \code{selection_records} or \code{fits}.
\item \code{\link[=aic_tab]{aic_tab()}} and \code{\link[=bic_tab]{bic_tab()}} refuse a cross-validation result, because
held-out scores are not a maximized likelihood.
}
}

\section{Conditional choice results (multiScaleR_choice_cv)}{
\code{\link[=cross_validate_clogit]{cross_validate_clogit()}} scores the probability of the used alternative
within each held-out choice set, and the elements change accordingly.
\itemize{
\item \code{predictions} has one row per alternative. \code{observed} is 1 for the used
alternative and 0 for the available ones, \code{choice} is the set ID, and
\code{trials} and \code{weight} are 1. \code{linear_predictor} is uncentered, so only
differences within a set matter. \code{prediction} is the probability of the
alternative within its set (the probabilities of a set sum to 1), and
\code{log_probability} is its natural log. Only the used alternative is
\code{eligible}, and its \code{neg_log_score} is the negative log probability of
the choice. Log scores are computed directly on the log scale, so they
stay finite when a printed probability rounds to zero.
\item \code{summary} and \code{metrics} hold \code{neg_log_score} only, averaged over choice
sets, with \code{unit = "choice_set"}. \code{n_assessment}, \code{n_scored}, and
\code{weight_sum} count sets, and \code{n_alternatives} counts the alternative
rows. A model without covariates or offsets gives each of k
alternatives a probability of 1/k and scores log(k), for example 1.099
for three alternatives.
\item \code{fold_results} counts alternative rows, and \code{targets} adds \code{choice}.
\item \code{diagnostics} holds \code{initial} and \code{native} (iteration count \code{iter} and
partial-likelihood \code{method}) and \code{scale}.
\item \code{selection_records} are version 2. Their \code{nobs} counts choice sets (one
event each), not alternatives, and they record the \code{method}.
\item \code{settings} gives \code{family = "conditional_choice"}, \code{link = "softmax"},
the \code{method}, and \code{backend = "clogit"} when no scales were estimated.
\item Its \code{print()} method reports the folds scored and prints \code{summary}.
}
}

\section{Spatial field results (multiScaleR_field_cv)}{
\code{\link[=cross_validate_field]{cross_validate_field()}} scores each held-out block jointly, so that
correlation among neighboring held-out observations counts.
\itemize{
\item \code{predictions} holds \code{fold}, \code{row_id}, \code{observed}, \code{prediction} (the
predicted response mean), \code{squared_error}, \code{absolute_error}, and
\code{eligible}, plus four variance columns.
\code{new_location_field_variance} is the field variance that would remain
even if the field were known exactly at the training locations, and
\code{fitted_training_field_variance} is the extra variance that arises
because the field there is itself estimated. Both are on the
linear-predictor scale and, for non-Gaussian families, describe a local
Gaussian approximation. \code{observation_noise_variance} (response scale)
is the observation variance, averaged over the field for non-Gaussian
families. \code{observation_predictive_SE} (response scale) is the standard
deviation of a new observation, combining the field and the noise. It is
a predictive SD, not the standard error of the mean.
\item \code{joint_scores} holds, for each fold, the block size \code{n} and the block's
\code{neg_log_score}. Gaussian blocks add the \code{quadratic} loss and
\code{log_determinant}. Laplace blocks add the analysis and combined log
densities, their latent-mode diagnostics, and the gap from the native
analysis likelihood.
\item \code{summary} has columns \code{metric}, \code{estimate}, \code{n_assessment}, \code{unit}, and
\code{complete}. The log score is the sum of the block scores divided by the
number of assessment observations, with unit
\code{"joint_block_density_per_observation"} or
\code{"joint_block_mass_per_observation"} (counts and 0/1 data). RMSE and MAE
have unit \code{"response"}. There is no \code{metrics} table, so use
\code{cv_tab(by_fold = TRUE)} for fold scores.
\item \code{training} holds the analysis \code{row_ids}, \code{parameters} (\code{field_SD}, the
correlation \code{range} in coordinate units, \code{observation_SD} or the NB2 or
Gamma dispersion, and parameter counts), \code{coefficients} (link scale),
and, with kernel inputs, \code{normalization}, \code{scale_est}, \code{bin_reference},
and the number of \code{refits}.
\item \code{selection_records} are always kept. Each holds \code{logLik}, \code{K},
\code{native_K}, \code{ecological_K}, \code{nobs}, \code{training_ids}, and hashes of the
training responses and coordinates.
\item \code{diagnostics} holds the native fit record of each fold, including the
covariance starts tried and the path of its audit file.
\item \code{targets} holds \code{row_id} and \code{observed}, \code{coordinates} holds \code{row_id},
\code{x}, and \code{y}, and \code{integrity_sha256} is a hash of the recorded results.
\code{\link[=cv_tab]{cv_tab()}} and \code{\link[=select_cv]{select_cv()}} refuse a result whose records were changed
after the run.
}
}

\section{Mixed model results (multiScaleR_mixed_cv)}{
\code{\link[=cross_validate_mixed]{cross_validate_mixed()}} scores all held-out rows of a group jointly,
because they share one unknown group effect.
\itemize{
\item \code{predictions} adds \code{group}, and \code{prediction} is the response mean
integrated over the group effect (the success probability per trial for
binomial data).
\item \code{joint_scores} holds, for each scored fold, a data frame with \code{group},
the number of held-out rows \code{n}, and the group's joint
\code{neg_log_score}.
\item In \code{summary} and \code{metrics}, the log score is the sum of the group scores
divided by the number of assessment rows (unit
\code{"joint_group_density_per_row"}). RMSE and MAE have unit \code{"response"},
or \code{"proportion"} for binomial data, where they compare the predicted
probability with the observed fraction of successes. The Brier score
has unit \code{"trial"}.
\item \code{training} holds the analysis \code{row_ids}, \code{coefficients} (link scale),
the random-effect \code{covariance}, the native \code{dispersion} (1 for Poisson
and binomial), and any fitted \code{scale_est}, \code{normalization}, and
\code{bin_reference}.
\item \code{selection_records} are always kept, with \code{logLik}, \code{K}, \code{native_K},
\code{ecological_K}, \code{nobs}, and the \code{analysis} row IDs.
\item \code{diagnostics} holds \code{convergence}, \code{positive_hessian}, \code{native_message},
and \code{scale_convergence}, and \code{integrity_sha256} protects the records as
for field results.
}
}

\section{Bayesian results (multiScaleR_bayes_cv)}{
\code{\link[=cross_validate_bayes]{cross_validate_bayes()}} scores held-out blocks with the posterior
predictive density, the only score in the package that averages over
parameter uncertainty. Its \code{summary} reports the joint negative log score
per observation, and its \code{predictions} hold posterior mean responses and
marginal row scores. It has no training likelihood, so \code{\link[=select_cv]{select_cv()}} does
not apply. See \code{\link[=cross_validate_bayes]{cross_validate_bayes()}} for its elements.
}

\examples{
data("landscape_counts")
data("surv_pts")
# Three spatial folds. The simulated coordinates are in meters.
folds <- spatial_cv_folds(crds(vect(surv_pts)), block_size = 1000, k = 3,
                          crs = 32617)
cv <- cross_validate_glm(counts ~ site, landscape_counts, folds,
                         family = "poisson", keep_selection = TRUE)
class(cv)
cv$complete           # TRUE: every fold was fitted and scored
cv$summary            # pooled scores, one row per metric, lower is better
cv$metrics[cv$metrics$metric == "rmse", ]   # the same score in each fold
head(cv$predictions)  # one row per held-out observation, with its losses
cv$fold_results       # rows fitted and held out, and the status of each fold
cv$training$fold1$coefficients   # log-link coefficients fitted in fold 1

# What select_cv() reads from each fold
cv$selection_records$fold1$data[c("logLik", "K", "nobs")]

# The pooled RMSE is the square root of the mean squared error over all
# held-out rows, not the mean of the three fold RMSEs
sqrt(mean(cv$predictions$rmse))
}
